---
const asks = [
  { name: "Speak MCP or run a CLI", body: "Run tests in environments, submit, ask the judge, all over MCP. Any agent that can run a shell command can use Onus." },
  { name: "Work inside its token", body: "And ask for more through escalation, with evidence." },
  { name: "Submit evidence with every change", body: "Intent, the semantic report, the tests behind it." },
];
---

<section class="chapter" id="agents" data-phase="6" aria-labelledby="agents-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="chapter-copy wide">
      <h2 class="display-l" id="agents-title">Any agent. Same rules.</h2>
      <p class="body-l">
        Onus isn't an agent and doesn't pick one for you. Codex, Claude Code or one you built yourself: each gets
        the same scopes, lanes and review, and earns its own track record. CI keeps that record from what happens to its pull requests: merged, reverted, or the cause of an incident.
      </p>
      <ul class="asks">
        {
          asks.map((a) => (
            <li>
              <h3 class="title">{a.name}</h3>
              <p class="muted">{a.body}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="display-s swap">Swap every agent overnight and nothing in Onus changes. Only the track records start fresh.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .wide {
    max-width: 52rem;
  }

  .wide .body-l {
    max-width: 36rem;
  }

  .asks {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 14rem), 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  .asks li {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    align-content: start;
    padding-top: var(--space-4);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--ink);
  }

  .asks .title {
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
  }

  .swap {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    max-width: 28ch;
  }
</style>
